Changelog: 08-10-2026 04:14:17

Improved readme with expanding pictures.

Changelog: 08-10-2026 01:43:15

Fixed failing test for bc250_governor/instance.py:
The weak point was this sequence in forward(): waitForBytesWritten() means the data was handed off to the socket,
not necessarily that the peer has processed it. Immediately disconnecting can race with the server's Qt event loop.
The GitHub Linux runner is exposing that race.
There was also a second issue: InstanceServer doesn't retain the accepted sockets. Qt ownership normally handles this,
but retaining them explicitly makes the lifetime and buffering behavior much more robust.

The current implementation creates a new QLocalSocket for every call and immediately tears it down. That's perfectly
plausible code, but it makes the test dependent on timing.
The revised implementation makes the contract explicit:
- forward() doesn't report success until the payload has been written.
- It waits for the disconnect to complete.
- The server keeps accepted sockets alive.
- Each socket is removed only after its disconnected signal.
- Multiple simultaneous/sequential requests are handled independently.

Changelog: 08-10-2026 01:04:08

The test test_forward_reaches_listener is failing because the received messages don't match expectations. 
The test expects: ["show", "profile Quiet night"] But it's only receiving: ["show"]
The second message "profile Quiet night" is not being received by the listener.
The issue is a timing problem. The _pump(ms=50) function only processes events for 50 milliseconds, which 
may not be sufficient for both messages to be received and processed by the socket listener, especially 
under CI/CD environments where system load varies.

Fix: Increase the event processing time or add a retry loop to ensure both messages are received:

Changelog: 07-10-2026 13:33:05

GUI translations, like the sibling bc250-cu-bisect GUI has. Every user-visible string is wrapped in self.tr()
(QCoreApplication.translate() outside QObject subclasses, QT_TRANSLATE_NOOP for constants built at import time
and translated where they are shown), and the app ships German, Spanish, French, Italian, Japanese, Polish,
Russian and Simplified Chinese: 585 source strings per language. QTranslator loads the system locale at startup
and Qt's own qtbase_<lang>.qm translates the standard dialog buttons; --lang de|es|fr|it|ja|pl|ru|zh forces one,
anything else keeps the English original.
Runtime values moved from f-strings to numbered %1/%2 placeholders, filled by the new fmt() helper in
__init__.py (PyQt6 returns tr() as a plain str, which has no QString.arg()), so a translation may reorder them.
Text that is compared, used as a key, written to config.toml, a CSV or the diagnostics report, or passed to a
subprocess stays English: the preset combo and the load-tool combo now carry the English name as item data and
are read with currentData()/findData() instead of their shown text, the nav lookup for hidden pages uses the
page map instead of the label, and the journal view tracks "following" with a flag instead of matching the label.
New tools/i18n/: build.py collects the strings with pylupdate6, expands each class context to its subclasses
(PyQt resolves self.tr() with the context of the instance's class, so an inherited call looks up the subclass
name), writes bc250_governor/translations/bc250_governor_<lang>.ts from the flat per-language catalog
tools/i18n/<lang>.json and compiles the .qm files; --check reports missing, unused or broken placeholders.
Compiling needs lrelease (Qt) or pyside6-lrelease (pip install PySide6-Essentials) at build time only.
README has a new "Languages" section.

Changelog: 07-10-2026 11:23:08

Squashed "Per game" box — the Performance page is taller than the window and had no scroll area, so Qt compressed e
very row. Every page is now hosted in a frameless QScrollArea (scrollable()); a too‑short window scrolls instead
of crushing the controls. Page lookups switched from stack.indexOf(page) to a page_rows map since the stack now holds
the wrappers.
Window opening with the title bar off‑screen — after restoreGeometry, keep_on_screen() clamps the frame into the
screen's available area (and shrinks it if it's larger than the screen), so the close button is always reachable.
Drag anywhere — mousePressEvent calls windowHandle().startSystemMove(), so left‑dragging any empty spot
(header, margins, labels) moves the window via the compositor; works on both X11 and Wayland.
Clicks on buttons/inputs are unaffected.

